# goal
string model_name  # 'act', 'diffusion_policy', 'gr00t', 'pi0' ...

# Language instruction for the policy. Every stack bridged here except ACT is
# language-conditioned, and without this field the instruction lives only inside
# the bridge, so a behaviour tree has no way to say what the task is. The bridge
# forwards it to the policy server; the controller only carries it.
string task

# The bridge's inference rate [Hz]. Must be finite and > 0 or the goal is
# rejected; the controller only logs it. It does not time a chunk: a chunk with
# ActionChunk.control_dt <= 0 is rejected, not timed from this.
float32 inference_frequency

# Least quiet time on the chunk stream after the last accepted chunk before the
# controller stops following it and holds [s]. <= 0 uses the controller's
# configured default. The buffered horizon counts as liveness: one that outlasts
# this moves the hold to its end, at most stream_horizon_credit_sec (controller
# parameter, 1 s) after the last chunk. The hold comes at
#   max(last chunk + stream_timeout,
#       min(horizon end, last chunk + stream_horizon_credit_sec)),
# so a policy that re-infers every 0.5 s with a 1 s horizon never holds, and a
# dead one is followed open-loop for at most that credit. This is a STREAM
# timeout, not a goal budget: a goal budget cannot tell a long successful task
# from a policy that died early, and while it runs down the arm sits frozen at a
# mid-motion waypoint.
float64 stream_timeout
---
# result
bool is_completed
# Empty on success. On failure this carries the reason the goal did not
# complete, so operator clients can print it instead of a bare failure.
string message
---
# feedback
# Playback time left in the buffered horizon [s]. Replaces the pre-v2
# percent_complete, which was never published and could not be defined anyway --
# a policy rollout has no known endpoint. A bridge paces its next observation off
# this (LeRobot's chunk_size_threshold rule).
float32 remaining_horizon_sec
uint32 chunks_accepted
uint32 chunks_rejected
